{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call-a38d8105-3bad-4e60-adff-72ba2b4e7a29","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# prompt.yaml\n2\t#\n3\t# Fill this in BEFORE you start an AI session. The agent reads it first, so the\n4\t# clearer it is, the fewer follow-up questions and the less rework.\n5\t#\n6\t# How to use it:\n7\t#   1. Fill what you know. Delete the example lines (they start with \"e.g.\").\n8\t#   2. Leave a field empty (\"\" or []) if it doesn't apply. Empty is fine.\n9\t#   3. Short and concrete beats long and vague. One line per item.\n10\t#\n11\t# After the PR merges, CI resets this file back to the blank template, so every\n12\t# new task starts clean. Don't keep history here, git already has it.\n13\t\n14\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n15\t# title: a few words, like a PR title. What would you call this task?\n16\t# e.g. \"Show future hosted events on the host profile\"\n17\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n18\ttitle: \"\"\n19\t\n20\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n21\t# type: pick ONE. It tells the agent which skill and checklist to follow.\n22\t#   feature   new behaviour that didn't exist\n23\t#   bug       something is broken (the agent fixes it, not just explains it)\n24\t#   refactor  same behaviour, cleaner code\n25\t#   perf      too slow, needs to be faster at production scale\n26\t#   review    just look at the changes and report, don't edit\n27\t#   question  explain how something works, don't edit\n28\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n29\ttype: feature\n30\t\n31\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n32\t# apps: which parts of the repo this touches. Anything not listed stays untouched.\n33\t#   server | web | crm | mobile | shared\n34\t# e.g. [server, web]\n35\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n36\tapps: []\n37\t\n38\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n39\t# objective: the outcome, in one or two sentences, from the user's point of view.\n40\t# Say WHAT should be true when it's done, not HOW to code it.\n41\t#\n42\t# bad:  \"Change the where clause in host.repository\"\n43\t# good: \"A host's upcoming events stay visible on their public profile until\n44\t#        the event's end time has passed.\"\n45\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n46\tobjective: \"\"\n47\t\n48\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n49\t# context: what the agent can't guess. Current behaviour, what you already\n50\t# tried, the bug report, a link to the Figma frame or the PR. Skip if obvious.\n51\t# e.g.\n52\t#   - \"Profile page only lists events where date <= now, so future ones vanish.\"\n53\t#   - \"Figma: https://figma.com/design/...\"\n54\t#   - \"Reported by support, happens on mobile web only.\"\n55\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n56\tcontext:\n57\t  - \"\"\n58\t\n59\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n60\t# scope: the files, screens or endpoints the agent SHOULD work on.\n61\t# Give paths or names if you know them, plain words if you don't.\n62\t# e.g.\n63\t#   - \"apps/server/src/repositories/host-profile.repository.ts\"\n64\t#   - \"the public profile page, 'Currently happening' section\"\n65\t#   - \"GET /api/v1/hosts/:username\"\n66\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n67\tscope:\n68\t  - \"\"\n69\t\n70\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n71\t# out_of_scope: things the agent must NOT touch, even if they look related.\n72\t# e.g.\n73\t#   - \"apps/crm and apps/mobile\"\n74\t#   - \"BrunoCollection files\"\n75\t#   - \"the payout logic, it is being rewritten separately\"\n76\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n77\tout_of_scope:\n78\t  - \"\"\n79\t\n80\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n81\t# constraints: rules the solution has to follow. Product rules, tech limits,\n82\t# style choices. The repo's AGENTS.md already applies, only add task-specific ones.\n83\t# e.g.\n84\t#   - \"No new dependencies, reuse what's installed\"\n85\t#   - \"Money stays integer paise\"\n86\t#   - \"No visual changes, logic only\"\n87\t#   - \"Must stay backwards compatible with the current mobile app\"\n88\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n89\tconstraints:\n90\t  - \"\"\n91\t\n92\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n93\t# acceptance: how we know it's done. Write each as something you can check,\n94\t# ideally \"when I do X, I see Y\".\n95\t# e.g.\n96\t#   - \"Host with an event starting tomorrow: it shows under Upcoming, not Past\"\n97\t#   - \"An event that ended an hour ago moves to Past\"\n98\t#   - \"tsc and lint pass, existing Playwright specs for profile still pass\"\n99\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n100\tacceptance:\n101\t  - \"\"\n102\t\n103\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n104\t# approval: things the agent must ASK you about before doing them. If it's\n105\t# listed here, the agent stops and asks, it never assumes.\n106\t# e.g.\n107\t#   - \"Any visual or copy change\"\n108\t#   - \"Adding a database migration\"\n109\t#   - \"Writing a spec or plan\"\n110\t#   - \"Committing and opening the PR\"\n111\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n112\tapproval:\n113\t  - \"\"\n114\t\n115\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n116\t# delivery: what you want at the end.\n117\t#   commit:  ask | parts | none      (parts = several small focused commits)\n118\t#   pr:      none | open-to-staging  (never merged by the agent)\n119\t#   branch:  leave empty to let the agent create one from the title\n120\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n121\tdelivery:\n122\t  commit: ask\n123\t  pr: none\n124\t  branch: \"\"\n125\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dd825012-52aa-4f5b-b0f6-761df9f383e4","uuid":"6a19c994-4bf2-4107-9b46-f7d54a2e8f30","timestamp":"2026-11-20T19:04:27.220Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/l9/28lkl3090p9dzbh6jvml0kkr0000gn/T/zerd-duel-zpf_oqz9/tunyt/prompt.yaml","content":"# prompt.yaml\n#\n# Fill this in BEFORE you start an AI session. The agent reads it first, so the\n# clearer it is, the fewer follow-up questions and the less rework.\n#\n# How to use it:\n#   1. Fill what you know. Delete the example lines (they start with \"e.g.\").\n#   2. Leave a field empty (\"\" or []) if it doesn't apply. Empty is fine.\n#   3. Short and concrete beats long and vague. One line per item.\n#\n# After the PR merges, CI resets this file back to the blank template, so every\n# new task starts clean. Don't keep history here, git already has it.\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# title: a few words, like a PR title. What would you call this task?\n# e.g. \"Show future hosted events on the host profile\"\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ntitle: \"\"\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# type: pick ONE. It tells the agent which skill and checklist to follow.\n#   feature   new behaviour that didn't exist\n#   bug       something is broken (the agent fixes it, not just explains it)\n#   refactor  same behaviour, cleaner code\n#   perf      too slow, needs to be faster at production scale\n#   review    just look at the changes and report, don't edit\n#   question  explain how something works, don't edit\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ntype: feature\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# apps: which parts of the repo this touches. Anything not listed stays untouched.\n#   server | web | crm | mobile | shared\n# e.g. [server, web]\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\napps: []\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# objective: the outcome, in one or two sentences, from the user's point of view.\n# Say WHAT should be true when it's done, not HOW to code it.\n#\n# bad:  \"Change the where clause in host.repository\"\n# good: \"A host's upcoming events stay visible on their public profile until\n#        the event's end time has passed.\"\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nobjective: \"\"\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# context: what the agent can't guess. Current behaviour, what you already\n# tried, the bug report, a link to the Figma frame or the PR. Skip if obvious.\n# e.g.\n#   - \"Profile page only lists events where date <= now, so future ones vanish.\"\n#   - \"Figma: https://figma.com/design/...\"\n#   - \"Reported by support, happens on mobile web only.\"\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncontext:\n  - \"\"\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# scope: the files, screens or endpoints the agent SHOULD work on.\n# Give paths or names if you know them, plain words if you don't.\n# e.g.\n#   - \"apps/server/src/repositories/host-profile.repository.ts\"\n#   - \"the public profile page, 'Currently happening' section\"\n#   - \"GET /api/v1/hosts/:username\"\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nscope:\n  - \"\"\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# out_of_scope: things the agent must NOT touch, even if they look related.\n# e.g.\n#   - \"apps/crm and apps/mobile\"\n#   - \"BrunoCollection files\"\n#   - \"the payout logic, it is being rewritten separately\"\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nout_of_scope:\n  - \"\"\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# constraints: rules the solution has to follow. Product rules, tech limits,\n# style choices. The repo's AGENTS.md already applies, only add task-specific ones.\n# e.g.\n#   - \"No new dependencies, reuse what's installed\"\n#   - \"Money stays integer paise\"\n#   - \"No visual changes, logic only\"\n#   - \"Must stay backwards compatible with the current mobile app\"\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nconstraints:\n  - \"\"\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# acceptance: how we know it's done. Write each as something you can check,\n# ideally \"when I do X, I see Y\".\n# e.g.\n#   - \"Host with an event starting tomorrow: it shows under Upcoming, not Past\"\n#   - \"An event that ended an hour ago moves to Past\"\n#   - \"tsc and lint pass, existing Playwright specs for profile still pass\"\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nacceptance:\n  - \"\"\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# approval: things the agent must ASK you about before doing them. If it's\n# listed here, the agent stops and asks, it never assumes.\n# e.g.\n#   - \"Any visual or copy change\"\n#   - \"Adding a database migration\"\n#   - \"Writing a spec or plan\"\n#   - \"Committing and opening the PR\"\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\napproval:\n  - \"\"\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# delivery: what you want at the end.\n#   commit:  ask | parts | none      (parts = several small focused commits)\n#   pr:      none | open-to-staging  (never merged by the agent)\n#   branch:  leave empty to let the agent create one from the title\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndelivery:\n  commit: ask\n  pr: none\n  branch: \"\"\n","numLines":125,"startLine":1,"totalLines":125}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"call-a38d8105-3bad-4e60-adff-72ba2b4e7a29","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call-e1b8783f-6855-40d0-98e3-1705c3e6382e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AGENTS.md — apps/server\n2\t\n3\tThe short, practical version of the rules for working inside `apps/server`. The root `AGENTS.md`\n4\tis the full rulebook and wins if the two ever disagree.\n5\t\n6\t> **Read the root \"Absolute Rules\" first.** In short: never read a `.env*` file, and never run SQL\n7\t> or any DB command. Write the query and hand it to the user. That includes everything in\n8\t> `apps/server/scripts/`, which is mostly one-off repair scripts that write to the DB.\n9\t\n10\tExpress 5 + TypeScript API. Prisma 7 on PostgreSQL, Redis, BullMQ, Zod, Winston. It runs as two\n11\tprocesses from one image: the HTTP API (`src/bin.ts`) and the BullMQ workers\n12\t(`src/workers/index.worker.ts`). AI generation happens inside the workers, there is no separate\n13\tAI service.\n14\t\n15\t## Which skill to read first\n16\t\n17\t| You're about to…                                         | Read                                                  |\n18\t| -------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n19\t| Touch any route, controller, service, repo or type       | `.claude/skills/api-conventions/SKILL.md`             |\n20\t| Write a list endpoint, Prisma query, cache or worker     | `docs/skills/tunyt-performance.md`                    |\n21\t| Chase a bug, a 500, a failing spec or a stuck job        | `docs/skills/tunyt-debugging.md`                      |\n22\t| Change payment, auth or sensitive-data code              | `.claude/skills/security-testing-compliance/SKILL.md` |\n23\t| Say \"done\"                                               | `docs/skills/tunyt-verification.md`                   |\n24\t\n25\t## The layer rule\n26\t\n27\t`Route → Controller → Service → Repository → Database`. Never skip a layer.\n28\t\n29\t| Layer      | Does                                                                 | Never does                    |\n30\t| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n31\t| Route      | paths, middleware, binds a controller                                | logic                         |\n32\t| Controller | parses input with Zod, calls one service, replies via `SuccessHandler` | business rules, Prisma        |\n33\t| Service    | business logic, orchestrates repositories, throws typed errors       | `req`/`res`, Prisma           |\n34\t| Repository | Prisma calls inside `wrapDatabaseOperation(...)`                     | business rules                |\n35\t\n36\tA handful of services (about 28 call sites) still call `prisma.*` directly. That's old debt. Move\n37\tthem into a repository when you're already in the file, and never add a new one.\n38\t\n39\t## House rules\n40\t\n41\t- **Schema first.** The Zod schema lives in `src/types/<domain>.types.ts`, the TS type is\n42\t  `z.infer<typeof Schema>`. No new files under `validators/`.\n43\t- **Errors are thrown**, never returned. Use the classes in `utils/errors.util.ts`\n44\t  (`ValidationError`, `NotFoundError`, `ForbiddenError`, `ConflictError`, ...). They all extend\n45\t  `AppError`.\n46\t- **`wrapDatabaseOperation` on every Prisma call.** It logs the raw Prisma error and throws a\n47\t  sanitized one, so prod logs and HTTP bodies stay clean. When you debug, look in the server log,\n48\t  not the response body.\n49\t- **Controllers** use `asyncHandler(fn, \"Label\")`, then `requireUser(req)` first if the route is\n50\t  behind auth. No `try/catch` that just re-throws.\n51\t- **External calls** (email, SMS, payments, OpenAI) go through a circuit breaker from\n52\t  `utils/circuit-breaker.util.ts`. Breaker state is per process, so the API and the worker each\n53\t  have their own.\n54\t- **Money is integer paise** (`utils/money.util.ts`). No float math on rupees.\n55\t- **Pricing has one source of truth:** `computeSeatPricing` (`utils/fee-pricing.util.ts`) for fees\n56\t  and `computeDiscountSplit` (`utils/discount-pricing.util.ts`) for early-bird discounts. Never\n57\t  hand-roll `platformfee + subtotal * perc` or read `discountedPrice` directly.\n58\t- **Equal splits** are done in integer paise with largest-remainder rounding, never\n59\t  `Math.ceil(total / n)` per person.\n60\t- **Counts must be real.** \"N sold\" comes from tickets with `status = SUCCESS`, not from a\n61\t  denormalized counter. \"Has this user bought\" is checked on `Ticket.userId`, not `Booking.userId`\n62\t  (in team bookings the payer and the ticket holder are different people).\n63\t- **Tenant scoping goes in the `WHERE` clause**, not in an `if` after the fetch. A post-fetch check\n64\t  silently does nothing if the column is never populated.\n65\t- **PATCH endpoints built from `Schema.partial()`** must use `parsePartialUpdate`\n66\t  (`utils/partial-parse.util.ts`), because `.partial()` keeps `.default()` values and would reset\n67\t  fields the caller never sent.\n68\t- **Webhooks** go through `runWebhookOnce` (`utils/webhook-dedup.util.ts`), signatures are compared\n69\t  with `crypto.timingSafeEqual`, and secrets are required keys in `config/index.ts`.\n70\t- **`jwt.verify`** always gets `{ clockTolerance: 10, algorithms: [\"HS256\"] }`.\n71\t- **No provider call inside a Prisma `$transaction`**, and no unbounded `findMany` or `Promise.all`\n72\t  fan-out over DB calls.\n73\t- **Cache keys** look like `cache:{type}:{identifier}`, and every write path invalidates them.\n74\t- **Logging:** Winston, with whatever ids you have (`bookingId`, `userId`, `eventId`). Only a few\n75\t  services thread a `requestId` through today, so don't claim every log line has one.\n76\t- **Config, not `process.env`.** Read settings from `config/index.ts`, which validates them on\n77\t  startup. Don't add a new direct `process.env` read when the config already has the value.\n78\t\n79\t## Rate limits and check-in\n80\t\n81\t- Redis-backed `express-rate-limit`. The AI endpoints (`aiEventCreationLimiter`) allow 10 calls per\n82\t  5 minutes per IP, which is why the AI workers run at concurrency 2.\n83\t- `/api/v1/check-in/*` and `/api/v1/slot-check-in/*` skip the global burst and general limiters,\n84\t  because a venue gate scans many guests from one NAT'd IP. `/api/v1/checker/*` is deliberately\n85\t  **not** exempt (it carries `/checker/login`). Sustained 429s feed `trackSuspiciousIP`, which\n86\t  blocks the whole IP for 15 minutes, so a gate that hits the limiter goes dark.\n87\t- The QR scanner uses `POST /api/v1/check-in/batch` (`{ ticketIds: string[1..50], quantities? }`),\n88\t  registered before `POST /check-in/:ticketId` so Express doesn't read \"batch\" as a ticket id.\n89\t\n90\t## Health, metrics and env\n91\t\n92\t- `GET /api/v1/health` checks DB and Redis. `GET /api/v1/metrics` is Prometheus text,\n93\t  `GET /metrics/json` is JSON.\n94\t- Env names only, never values (root Absolute Rules). The non-obvious ones: without\n95\t  `OPENAI_API_KEY` the AI worker retries forever and the draft status never resolves;\n96\t  `OPENAI_API_BASE_URL` can point at any OpenAI-compatible endpoint; `AI_DEFAULT_MODEL` defaults to\n97\t  `gpt-4o-mini`; both `RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET` and `RAZORPAY_SUBSCRIPTION_WEBHOOK_SECRET` are\n98\t  required; `FEATURE_SLOT_BOOKING` gates the venue-slot vertical.\n99\t\n100\t## Adding an endpoint\n101\t\n102\t1. Route in `routes/v1/<domain>.route.ts`, registered in `routes/v1/index.ts`. A literal path\n103\t   (`/check-in/batch`) must be registered before a param path (`/check-in/:ticketId`).\n104\t2. Controller → service → repository.\n105\t3. Zod schema in `types/<domain>.types.ts`.\n106\t4. Pick the right auth middleware from the table in the `api-conventions` skill. Don't hand-check\n107\t   tokens or roles in a controller.\n108\t5. A Bruno `.yml` in `BrunoCollection/<Domain>/`. That folder is at the **repo root**.\n109\t\n110\t## Workers, queues and AI\n111\t\n112\t- Queues are defined in `src/lib/queues.ts` (about 35). Each has one `src/workers/<name>.worker.ts`\n113\t  that `workers/index.worker.ts` imports. Use bounded `removeOnComplete` / `removeOnFail`, and put\n114\t  an idempotency key in the payload of any job that sends something or moves money.\n115\t- `pnpm dev` only starts the API. Without `pnpm run:worker` next to it, queued jobs (emails, AI\n116\t  drafts, refunds) never finish.\n117\t- AI has two flows, **event draft** and **event assistant**. The layout is `lib/ai/` (pure, no\n118\t  Prisma or Express), `services/ai/<flow>/` (enqueue service for HTTP, generation service for the\n119\t  worker) and `workers/event-draft.worker.ts` / `event-assistant.worker.ts`. The provider is only\n120\t  reached through `lib/ai/shared/client.ts`. Details in `services/ai/README.md`.\n121\t- Run `sanitizeUserPrompt(...)` on every user prompt before it reaches a prompt builder.\n122\t- The assistant's tools are read-only. Keep it that way.\n123\t\n124\t## Database and migrations\n125\t\n126\tYou edit files, the user touches the database.\n127\t\n128\t- You may edit `prisma/schema.prisma` and write migration SQL files, and run `npx prisma generate`\n129\t  (no DB connection). You may **not** run `migrate dev | deploy | status | resolve | reset`,\n130\t  `db push`, `studio` or anything that connects. Give the user the exact command instead.\n131\t- Applied migrations are immutable. Fix forward with a new one.\n132\t- A migration and the `schema.prisma` models it creates ship in the same commit.\n133\t- Never `db push` against a shared DB, never hand-apply SQL to prod. Either one writes schema\n134\t  changes with no ledger row, and from then on every `migrate dev` asks for a reset.\n135\t- A row in `_prisma_migrations` is not proof the SQL ran (`migrate resolve --applied` records a\n136\t  migration without running it). Ask the user to check the objects exist before you rely on them.\n137\t- A new filter in a query needs an index. On a busy table (`Booking`, `Ticket`,\n138\t  `UserActivityEvent`) the migration uses `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`\n139\t  (`docs/db-saturation-runbook.md`).\n140\t- Nothing in CI runs `migrate deploy`, so when you finish a schema change, say plainly that the\n141\t  user has to run `migrate status` then `migrate deploy` against prod themselves.\n142\t- `src/generated/prisma/` is generated output. Don't hand-edit it.\n143\t\n144\t## Naming\n145\t\n146\tNew files are `kebab-case` with a suffix: `booking.service.ts`, `event.repository.ts`,\n147\t`auth.controller.ts`, `fee-pricing.util.ts`. A few older files are still PascalCase (`Buyers`,\n148\t`Dashboard`, `Segment`...). Those are legacy, don't copy them.\n149\t\n150\t## Commands\n151\t\n152\t```bash\n153\tpnpm dev              # API only, no workers\n154\tpnpm run:worker       # BullMQ workers, run next to dev\n155\tpnpm test             # Playwright logic specs in tests/, no browser (~75 specs)\n156\tnpx tsc --noEmit      # type check\n157\tpnpm build            # tsc + copies email templates\n158\tnpx prisma generate   # after schema.prisma changes (safe, no DB connection)\n159\t```\n160\t\n161\tTests are Playwright only, never vitest or jest. Don't write new specs during a fix round unless\n162\tthe user asks. Starting `pnpm dev` / `run:worker` connects to the DB, so ask before you boot them.\n163\t\n164\t## Before you say \"done\"\n165\t\n166\t1. `npx tsc --noEmit` is clean and `pnpm build` passes.\n167\t2. The existing specs that cover your change pass (`pnpm test`).\n168\t3. Bruno updated if an endpoint was added, changed or removed.\n169\t4. Security skill done if you touched payment, auth or sensitive data.\n170\t5. Any schema change comes with a migration file and a note telling the user to apply it.\n171\t6. No `.env*` read, no DB command run. Queries went to the user.\n172\t7. `graphify update .` from the repo root.\n173\t8. Ask the user before committing.\n174\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dd825012-52aa-4f5b-b0f6-761df9f383e4","uuid":"757025df-ba82-44d0-bb8b-3e079d8f3aa0","timestamp":"2026-11-20T19:04:27.721Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/l9/28lkl3090p9dzbh6jvml0kkr0000gn/T/zerd-duel-zpf_oqz9/tunyt/apps/server/AGENTS.md","content":"# AGENTS.md — apps/server\n\nThe short, practical version of the rules for working inside `apps/server`. The root `AGENTS.md`\nis the full rulebook and wins if the two ever disagree.\n\n> **Read the root \"Absolute Rules\" first.** In short: never read a `.env*` file, and never run SQL\n> or any DB command. Write the query and hand it to the user. That includes everything in\n> `apps/server/scripts/`, which is mostly one-off repair scripts that write to the DB.\n\nExpress 5 + TypeScript API. Prisma 7 on PostgreSQL, Redis, BullMQ, Zod, Winston. It runs as two\nprocesses from one image: the HTTP API (`src/bin.ts`) and the BullMQ workers\n(`src/workers/index.worker.ts`). AI generation happens inside the workers, there is no separate\nAI service.\n\n## Which skill to read first\n\n| You're about to…                                         | Read                                                  |\n| -------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| Touch any route, controller, service, repo or type       | `.claude/skills/api-conventions/SKILL.md`             |\n| Write a list endpoint, Prisma query, cache or worker     | `docs/skills/tunyt-performance.md`                    |\n| Chase a bug, a 500, a failing spec or a stuck job        | `docs/skills/tunyt-debugging.md`                      |\n| Change payment, auth or sensitive-data code              | `.claude/skills/security-testing-compliance/SKILL.md` |\n| Say \"done\"                                               | `docs/skills/tunyt-verification.md`                   |\n\n## The layer rule\n\n`Route → Controller → Service → Repository → Database`. Never skip a layer.\n\n| Layer      | Does                                                                 | Never does                    |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| Route      | paths, middleware, binds a controller                                | logic                         |\n| Controller | parses input with Zod, calls one service, replies via `SuccessHandler` | business rules, Prisma        |\n| Service    | business logic, orchestrates repositories, throws typed errors       | `req`/`res`, Prisma           |\n| Repository | Prisma calls inside `wrapDatabaseOperation(...)`                     | business rules                |\n\nA handful of services (about 28 call sites) still call `prisma.*` directly. That's old debt. Move\nthem into a repository when you're already in the file, and never add a new one.\n\n## House rules\n\n- **Schema first.** The Zod schema lives in `src/types/<domain>.types.ts`, the TS type is\n  `z.infer<typeof Schema>`. No new files under `validators/`.\n- **Errors are thrown**, never returned. Use the classes in `utils/errors.util.ts`\n  (`ValidationError`, `NotFoundError`, `ForbiddenError`, `ConflictError`, ...). They all extend\n  `AppError`.\n- **`wrapDatabaseOperation` on every Prisma call.** It logs the raw Prisma error and throws a\n  sanitized one, so prod logs and HTTP bodies stay clean. When you debug, look in the server log,\n  not the response body.\n- **Controllers** use `asyncHandler(fn, \"Label\")`, then `requireUser(req)` first if the route is\n  behind auth. No `try/catch` that just re-throws.\n- **External calls** (email, SMS, payments, OpenAI) go through a circuit breaker from\n  `utils/circuit-breaker.util.ts`. Breaker state is per process, so the API and the worker each\n  have their own.\n- **Money is integer paise** (`utils/money.util.ts`). No float math on rupees.\n- **Pricing has one source of truth:** `computeSeatPricing` (`utils/fee-pricing.util.ts`) for fees\n  and `computeDiscountSplit` (`utils/discount-pricing.util.ts`) for early-bird discounts. Never\n  hand-roll `platformfee + subtotal * perc` or read `discountedPrice` directly.\n- **Equal splits** are done in integer paise with largest-remainder rounding, never\n  `Math.ceil(total / n)` per person.\n- **Counts must be real.** \"N sold\" comes from tickets with `status = SUCCESS`, not from a\n  denormalized counter. \"Has this user bought\" is checked on `Ticket.userId`, not `Booking.userId`\n  (in team bookings the payer and the ticket holder are different people).\n- **Tenant scoping goes in the `WHERE` clause**, not in an `if` after the fetch. A post-fetch check\n  silently does nothing if the column is never populated.\n- **PATCH endpoints built from `Schema.partial()`** must use `parsePartialUpdate`\n  (`utils/partial-parse.util.ts`), because `.partial()` keeps `.default()` values and would reset\n  fields the caller never sent.\n- **Webhooks** go through `runWebhookOnce` (`utils/webhook-dedup.util.ts`), signatures are compared\n  with `crypto.timingSafeEqual`, and secrets are required keys in `config/index.ts`.\n- **`jwt.verify`** always gets `{ clockTolerance: 10, algorithms: [\"HS256\"] }`.\n- **No provider call inside a Prisma `$transaction`**, and no unbounded `findMany` or `Promise.all`\n  fan-out over DB calls.\n- **Cache keys** look like `cache:{type}:{identifier}`, and every write path invalidates them.\n- **Logging:** Winston, with whatever ids you have (`bookingId`, `userId`, `eventId`). Only a few\n  services thread a `requestId` through today, so don't claim every log line has one.\n- **Config, not `process.env`.** Read settings from `config/index.ts`, which validates them on\n  startup. Don't add a new direct `process.env` read when the config already has the value.\n\n## Rate limits and check-in\n\n- Redis-backed `express-rate-limit`. The AI endpoints (`aiEventCreationLimiter`) allow 10 calls per\n  5 minutes per IP, which is why the AI workers run at concurrency 2.\n- `/api/v1/check-in/*` and `/api/v1/slot-check-in/*` skip the global burst and general limiters,\n  because a venue gate scans many guests from one NAT'd IP. `/api/v1/checker/*` is deliberately\n  **not** exempt (it carries `/checker/login`). Sustained 429s feed `trackSuspiciousIP`, which\n  blocks the whole IP for 15 minutes, so a gate that hits the limiter goes dark.\n- The QR scanner uses `POST /api/v1/check-in/batch` (`{ ticketIds: string[1..50], quantities? }`),\n  registered before `POST /check-in/:ticketId` so Express doesn't read \"batch\" as a ticket id.\n\n## Health, metrics and env\n\n- `GET /api/v1/health` checks DB and Redis. `GET /api/v1/metrics` is Prometheus text,\n  `GET /metrics/json` is JSON.\n- Env names only, never values (root Absolute Rules). The non-obvious ones: without\n  `OPENAI_API_KEY` the AI worker retries forever and the draft status never resolves;\n  `OPENAI_API_BASE_URL` can point at any OpenAI-compatible endpoint; `AI_DEFAULT_MODEL` defaults to\n  `gpt-4o-mini`; both `RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET` and `RAZORPAY_SUBSCRIPTION_WEBHOOK_SECRET` are\n  required; `FEATURE_SLOT_BOOKING` gates the venue-slot vertical.\n\n## Adding an endpoint\n\n1. Route in `routes/v1/<domain>.route.ts`, registered in `routes/v1/index.ts`. A literal path\n   (`/check-in/batch`) must be registered before a param path (`/check-in/:ticketId`).\n2. Controller → service → repository.\n3. Zod schema in `types/<domain>.types.ts`.\n4. Pick the right auth middleware from the table in the `api-conventions` skill. Don't hand-check\n   tokens or roles in a controller.\n5. A Bruno `.yml` in `BrunoCollection/<Domain>/`. That folder is at the **repo root**.\n\n## Workers, queues and AI\n\n- Queues are defined in `src/lib/queues.ts` (about 35). Each has one `src/workers/<name>.worker.ts`\n  that `workers/index.worker.ts` imports. Use bounded `removeOnComplete` / `removeOnFail`, and put\n  an idempotency key in the payload of any job that sends something or moves money.\n- `pnpm dev` only starts the API. Without `pnpm run:worker` next to it, queued jobs (emails, AI\n  drafts, refunds) never finish.\n- AI has two flows, **event draft** and **event assistant**. The layout is `lib/ai/` (pure, no\n  Prisma or Express), `services/ai/<flow>/` (enqueue service for HTTP, generation service for the\n  worker) and `workers/event-draft.worker.ts` / `event-assistant.worker.ts`. The provider is only\n  reached through `lib/ai/shared/client.ts`. Details in `services/ai/README.md`.\n- Run `sanitizeUserPrompt(...)` on every user prompt before it reaches a prompt builder.\n- The assistant's tools are read-only. Keep it that way.\n\n## Database and migrations\n\nYou edit files, the user touches the database.\n\n- You may edit `prisma/schema.prisma` and write migration SQL files, and run `npx prisma generate`\n  (no DB connection). You may **not** run `migrate dev | deploy | status | resolve | reset`,\n  `db push`, `studio` or anything that connects. Give the user the exact command instead.\n- Applied migrations are immutable. Fix forward with a new one.\n- A migration and the `schema.prisma` models it creates ship in the same commit.\n- Never `db push` against a shared DB, never hand-apply SQL to prod. Either one writes schema\n  changes with no ledger row, and from then on every `migrate dev` asks for a reset.\n- A row in `_prisma_migrations` is not proof the SQL ran (`migrate resolve --applied` records a\n  migration without running it). Ask the user to check the objects exist before you rely on them.\n- A new filter in a query needs an index. On a busy table (`Booking`, `Ticket`,\n  `UserActivityEvent`) the migration uses `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`\n  (`docs/db-saturation-runbook.md`).\n- Nothing in CI runs `migrate deploy`, so when you finish a schema change, say plainly that the\n  user has to run `migrate status` then `migrate deploy` against prod themselves.\n- `src/generated/prisma/` is generated output. Don't hand-edit it.\n\n## Naming\n\nNew files are `kebab-case` with a suffix: `booking.service.ts`, `event.repository.ts`,\n`auth.controller.ts`, `fee-pricing.util.ts`. A few older files are still PascalCase (`Buyers`,\n`Dashboard`, `Segment`...). Those are legacy, don't copy them.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\npnpm dev              # API only, no workers\npnpm run:worker       # BullMQ workers, run next to dev\npnpm test             # Playwright logic specs in tests/, no browser (~75 specs)\nnpx tsc --noEmit      # type check\npnpm build            # tsc + copies email templates\nnpx prisma generate   # after schema.prisma changes (safe, no DB connection)\n```\n\nTests are Playwright only, never vitest or jest. Don't write new specs during a fix round unless\nthe user asks. Starting `pnpm dev` / `run:worker` connects to the DB, so ask before you boot them.\n\n## Before you say \"done\"\n\n1. `npx tsc --noEmit` is clean and `pnpm build` passes.\n2. The existing specs that cover your change pass (`pnpm test`).\n3. Bruno updated if an endpoint was added, changed or removed.\n4. Security skill done if you touched payment, auth or sensitive data.\n5. Any schema change comes with a migration file and a note telling the user to apply it.\n6. No `.env*` read, no DB command run. Queries went to the user.\n7. `graphify update .` from the repo root.\n8. Ask the user before committing.\n","numLines":174,"startLine":1,"totalLines":174}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"call-e1b8783f-6855-40d0-98e3-1705c3e6382e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
